ashes. We'll do that manually.\n\nLet's craft the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"import math\\ndef solve(case):\\n    matrix = case['matrix']\\n    n = len(matrix)\\n    if n == 0:\\n        return {'idf': [], 'row_degree': [], 'weighted_matrix': []}\\n    m = len(matrix[0])\\n    col_sums = [0]*m\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(m):\\n            col_sums[j] += matrix[i][j]\\n    idf = [math.log((n+1)/(col_sums[j]+1)) for j in range(m)]\\n    row_degree = [sum(row) for row in matrix]\\n    weighted_matrix = [[matrix[i][j] * idf[j] for j in range(m)] for i in range(n)]\\n    return {'idf': idf, 'row_degree': row_degree, 'weighted_matrix': weighted_matrix}\"\n}\n\nBut note: In the Python code, the line \"if n == 0:\" is followed by \"return {'idf': [], 'row_degree': [], 'weighted_matrix': []}\" indented correctly. In the JSON string, we must ensure that the newlines are represented as \\n and the indentation is with spaces. The above JSON string will have actual newlines if we write it literally, but in JSON, newlines are not allowed in strings without escaping. We must escape them as \\n. So we need to replace each newline in the source code with the two characters backslash and n. Also escape any backslashes. In the code, there is a backslash in the division? No. There's no backslash in the code except in the math.log line? No backslash. But the division is /, not backslash. So only need to escape newlines and maybe double quotes (but we used single quotes, so no). However, Python source also contains single quotes inside dict literals, which are fine in JSON string as they are not special characters. So we just need to replace newline with \\n.\n\nLet's produce the escaped string:\n\n\"import math\\ndef solve(case):\\n    matrix = case['matrix']\\n    n = len(matrix)\\n    if n == 0:\\n        return {'idf': [], 'row_degree': [], 'weighted_matrix': []}\\n    m = len(matrix[0])\\n    col_sums = [0]*m\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in